<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Solving log equation with different bases</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Solve the equation</p>
\[ \log_{{@b@}} x + \log_{{@b^2@}} x = {@k@}. \]
<p>\(x =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p>
<div>[[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p><em>Hint:</em> use the change of base formula to write both logarithms to base \({@b@}\).</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3>
<p>Change \(\log_{{@b^2@}} x\) to base \({@b@}\). Since \(\log_{{@b@}} {@b^2@} = 2\),</p>
\[ \log_{{@b^2@}} x = \frac{\log_{{@b@}} x}{\log_{{@b@}} {@b^2@}} = \frac{1}{2}\log_{{@b@}} x. \]
<p>Substitute:</p>
\[ \log_{{@b@}} x + \frac{1}{2}\log_{{@b@}} x = {@k@} \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \frac{3}{2}\log_{{@b@}} x = {@k@} \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \log_{{@b@}} x = {@p@}. \]
<p>In index form, \(x = {@b@}^{@p@} = {@ta1@}\). It is positive, so both logarithms are defined.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
